 First, notice $h<30.$ The key insight is that $3$ rows from the Crate $B$ are packed together. By vicinity, we mean the yellow 3-part line I've drawn. [asy]
draw(circle((10,10),10),black+linewidth(1));
draw(circle((30,10),10),black+linewidth(1));
draw(circle((20,27.5),10),black+linewidth(1));
draw(circle((15,37.5),10),black+linewidth(1));
draw(circle((25,37.5),10),black+linewidth(1));
draw(circle((20,10),10),black+linewidth(1));
draw(circle((30,10),10),black+linewidth(1));
draw(circle((20,37.5),10),black+linewidth(1));
draw((30,10)--(30,37.5),black+linewidth(1));
draw((29,10)--(31,10),black+linewidth(1));
draw((29,37.5)--(31,37.5),black+linewidth(1));
draw((10,10)--(10,37.5),black+linewidth(1));
draw((15,37.5)--(15,10),black+linewidth(1));
draw((19,37.5)--(21,37.5),black+linewidth(1));
draw((19,10)--(21,10),black+linewidth(1));[/asy] Surrounding a layer of pipes with more space around them is less efficient, so $3$ rows is maximally efficient. Each pipe has a radius of $5\text{ cm},$ so the centers of the pipes are $10\text{ cm}$ apart. Draw a line through the middle of Crate $B,$ as shown. Since the reason for this line is not important as long as two pipes are above and below it, I can ignore it for now. [/stem] <answer> $30-\displaystyle\frac{10\sqrt{3}}{3}.$[/answer] You are told $h<30,$ but \begin{align*} h&= \text{First row height+between third row and second row} \\ &=10+10\cos(60^\circ) \\ &=10+5\sqrt{3}\end{align*} units and thus is demonstrably equal to $30-\displaystyle\frac{10\sqrt{3}}{3}.$ [/solution]
